# Security Rules

Inspired by [matank001/cursor-security-rules](https://github.com/matank001/cursor-security-rules). Security takes precedence over convenience.

## Never expose secrets to the client

- `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is the **only** env prefix allowed in client bundles.
- Service-role / admin / signing keys go through `src/lib/env/server.ts` and must be imported only from modules that `import "server-only"`.
- Never log, print, or interpolate a secret into a response body, error message, or client-visible string.

```ts
// BAD — leaks server secret into client bundle
export const KEY = process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY;

// GOOD — server-only access
import "server-only";
import { serverEnv } from "@/lib/env/server";
const key = serverEnv.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY;
```

## All sensitive logic runs server-side

- Authentication, authorization, billing, tenant provisioning, webhook handling: **Server Actions / Route Handlers only**.
- Never trust a `tenant_id`, `userId`, or role flag sent from the client — re-derive from the session on every call.

## Validate every input at the server boundary

- Parse with Zod (or equivalent) at the entry of every Server Action and Route Handler.
- Reject extra fields; do not pass raw request bodies into queries.

```ts
const Input = z.object({ slug: z.string().min(1).max(64) });
export async function action(formData: FormData) {
  const { slug } = Input.parse(Object.fromEntries(formData));
  // ...
}
```

## Tenant isolation is mandatory

- Every tenant-scoped query must filter by `tenant_id` resolved from the session, never from the URL alone.
- Enforce the same constraint in Supabase RLS as a second line of defense.
- Cross-tenant operations require a distinct platform-admin role and audit logging.

## Role-based access control (default deny)

- Check permissions on every server entry point — do not rely solely on UI hiding controls.
- Centralize role checks; do not scatter `if (role === "admin")` across routes.

## No unsafe terminal commands without explanation

When proposing shell commands, especially destructive (`rm -rf`, `DROP`, `--force`, `migrate reset`) or that touch secrets/auth, always:

1. State **what** the command does.
2. State **why** it is needed now.
3. State **what it changes irreversibly**.

Never paste a one-liner that mutates state without that context.
